---
/**
 * The honest-limits block. Every chain page carries it.
 *
 * These statements are identical across the five pages on purpose. They are a
 * disclosure, not content, and the facts genuinely do not vary by chain: the
 * study is 30 items, none of our 22 datasets carries a calorie column, and
 * fast-food pricing is regional everywhere.
 *
 * The chain-specific part goes in the default slot.
 */
import { STUDY_SIZE, STUDY_CHAINS, CSV_PATH, PARENT_PATH } from "./fastfood";

interface Props {
  chain: string;
  itemCount: number;
  observed: string;
}

const { chain, itemCount, observed } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl" id="what-this-doesnt-cover">
  <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-4" style="color: var(--heading-color);">
    What this page doesn't cover
  </h2>

  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
    Worth being blunt about the edges of this, because a food-cost page that
    oversells itself is worth less than no page at all.
  </p>

  <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-3 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
    <li>
      <strong style="color:var(--heading-color);">It's {itemCount} items, not the {chain} menu.</strong>
      The whole study is {STUDY_SIZE} protein-notable items across {STUDY_CHAINS} chains, picked because
      they're the ones people actually order for protein. {chain} sells plenty of things that
      aren't here, and some of them might beat the winner above. We didn't price them, so we're
      not going to pretend to know.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong style="color:var(--heading-color);">There are no calorie numbers here, anywhere.</strong>
      Not one of our 22 datasets carries a calorie column, so this page doesn't have one and
      won't be getting one by borrowing figures from a nutrition site and quietly mixing them
      into an audited table. If you want calories per dollar, get them from a source that
      actually measured them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong style="color:var(--heading-color);">The prices are a dated snapshot, not your receipt.</strong>
      No chain publishes one national price. Every row above names its own basis: a national
      average from a named tracker, or the chain's own online menu on a stated day. The window
      for this page is {observed}. Your local drive-thru can sit a dollar or two either side of
      these numbers, and if you're reading this a year from now, assume it does.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong style="color:var(--heading-color);">Protein per dollar is one number, and it isn't the only one.</strong>
      This ranking says nothing about sodium, saturated fat, carbs, allergens, or whether the food
      is any good. It answers exactly one question: which order turns the most of your money into
      protein.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong style="color:var(--heading-color);">Combos, promos and app deals are out.</strong>
      Prices are for the item on its own where the row says so. Bundle it into a meal or catch a
      promo week and the maths moves, sometimes a lot.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--text-color);">
    You can check all of it. The
    <a href={CSV_PATH} class="underline font-semibold" style="color:#F29B30;">raw CSV</a>
    holds every row with its source and price basis, the
    <a href="/methodology/" class="underline font-semibold" style="color:#F29B30;">methodology page</a>
    explains the pricing rules, and the
    <a href={PARENT_PATH} class="underline font-semibold" style="color:#F29B30;">full {STUDY_SIZE}-item study</a>
    puts {chain} next to the other six chains.
    <slot />
  </p>
</section>
